TB invalidation on guest writes, four cuts (M9 track,
docs/tracks/m9-tcg-aarch64.md; found on Moto Racer 1997's in-engine intro
under TCG on the M1 Air, where the softmmu slow path and TB maintenance were
75 % of the vCPU thread):

1. tb_invalidate_phys_range() (the DMA / cpu_physical_memory_write side)
   rounded the first page's range down to the page start, invalidating every
   TB below the written bytes. The vAPIC option ROM keeps its TPR stubs (all
   of a Windows guest's IRQL fast paths) in the page below the VAPICState the
   APIC writes at every interrupt: thousands of retranslations a second on
   any XP guest, idle included. Upstream master still has it (2026-09-05).
2. A per-page byte range of the TBs on it (PageDesc code_lo/code_hi): a
   write outside it returns before the page collection (two g_tree lookups)
   and the list walk. Windows data pages carrying one stale TB (the vAPIC
   lock word, pool pages) are written tens of thousands of times a second.
3. The single-page path: a write that cannot hit a TB shared with a
   neighbouring page (PageDesc span_in_end / span_out_lo) is handled under
   the page's own spinlock, without the page collection; the precise-SMC
   exit unlocks that page itself. The current TB's g_tree lookup (precise
   SMC) is made only once a TB is actually hit.
4. No jump-cache flush per invalidated CF_PCREL TB (a 64 KiB memset each,
   then every TB entry misses until the cache warms): the TB already carries
   CF_INVALID, which tb_lookup() compares against, and its memory lives
   until the next tb_flush(), which flushes the caches itself. A
   self-modifying guest (the game patches its rasterizer's immediates per
   span) invalidates 20k TBs a second.

